fficient.\n\nWe have source length n, pattern length m. Allowed set A size k <= n. Need max deletions from A such that pattern subsequence remains.\n\nEquivalent: We need choose a subsequence of source matching pattern, avoiding deleted positions. We can delete allowed positions not used by chosen subsequence. To maximize deletions, choose a valid embedding of pattern in source that uses as few allowed positions as possible. Then deletions = k - (# allowed positions used by embedding). Because any allowed position not used can be deleted; allowed positions used must remain. Is that sufficient? If we choose an embedding (indices i1<...<im) that matches pattern, then deleting all allowed positions not in embedding leaves pattern subsequence (the embedding). Conversely, if a subset D of allowed positions is deletable, there exists an embedding avoiding D, so it uses only allowed positions not in D; deletions = k - used_allowed <= k - min_used_allowed. Thus max deletions = k - minimum number of allowed positions used by any subsequence embedding of pattern in source.\n\nSo problem reduces to: Given source, pattern, set A, find minimum number of positions from A used in a subsequence matching pattern. n,m <=3000. Need compute min allowed used.\n\nThis is like DP: dp[j] = min allowed used to match pattern prefix length j using processed source prefix. Iterate source chars, update j descending. If char matches pattern[j-1], new = dp[j-1] + (1 if i in A else 0). dp[0]=0. Answer k - dp[m]. Complexity O(n*m) = 9e6, fine in Python maybe okay. Need optimize. n=3000, m=3000 => 9 million updates, okay. But need careful with list and membership. Use boolean array is_target length n. dp length m+1 with INF. For each i, c=source[i], cost=1 if target else 0. Need update j from min(i+1,m) down to 1 if c==pattern[j-1]. Could be O(n*m) worst 9M, fine. But Python nested loops with if char match maybe okay. 9M is small. But if we loop all j for each i, 9M, okay. Need ensure descending to 